   "turn": 12,
   "response_text": "Now let me review the configuration in more detail to spot any issues with hyperparameters:\n\nBased on my investigation, I've found the following:\n\n1. **Final Validation Accuracy**: The run achieved a final validation accuracy of 0.8175 (epoch 19), which is **significantly below** the healthy range of 0.8538\u20130.8599.\n\n2. **Training Loss**: The training loss remains high throughout training (stays around 0.68-0.69), suggesting the model isn't learning effectively.\n\n3. **Configuration**: Looking at the config:\n   - Learning rate: 0.01 \n   - Batch size: 256\n   - Optimizer: Adam\n   - Weight decay: 0.0001\n   - Label noise fraction: 0.42 (this is high!)\n   - Model: MLP with hidden_dims [64, 32]\n\n4. **Key Issue - Label Noise**: The configuration includes `data.label_noise_fraction: 0.42`, which means **42% of the training labels are flipped**. This is extremely high and would severely degrade model performance. The code at line 138-141 in train.py shows this noise is applied:\n   ```python\n   if noise_frac > 0:\n       flip_idx = nested_prefix_indices(len(y_train_np), noise_frac)\n       y_train_np[flip_idx] = 1.0 - y_train_np[flip_idx]\n   ```\n\nThe problem is that the label_noise_fraction of 0.42 is far too high for a training run. This parameter appears to be intended for data corruption experiments rather than normal training. Healthy runs should have a label_noise_fraction of 0.0 (no noise).\n\nLet me verify this is the issue by checking if this parameter is anomalously high:",
